I've always wondered about this. As seen above, sliming changes a pet
into a green slime, but it doesn't die - it can still go on playing.
After a *player*, however, gets changed into a green slime by sliming,
he or she is considered to be "dead" and the game is over (although in
a bones file the player will appear not as a ghost but rather as the
green slime he or she changed into - so obviously "dead" is the wrong
term here.) Why not just allow the player to continue the game as a
green slime? Admittedly, green slimes can't do much, but if you find a
wand of polymorph/eat a chameleon/step on a polymorph trap, you might
be able to change into a much more useful form.
(Note: the sliming would then become just like a polymorph, but a
"permanent" one, meaning that you wouldn't change back into your
original form after a certain number of turns as in standard
polymorph, and if you died in green slime form that would be it (you
wouldn't revert to human form.))

